4. DEFLECT PROJECTILES

During Deflect’s active frames only, its active hitbox can intersect hostile projectiles.

Normal attacks must not automatically gain this property.

Make projectile deflection an explicit capability such as:

deflectProjectiles: true

For this task, only the new Deflect actions for 0001 and 0002 should opt into it unless an existing move already explicitly has equivalent semantics.

When an active Deflect hitbox contacts an enemy projectile:

1. The projectile must not damage the Deflecting fighter on that simulation step.
2. The projectile changes owner to the Deflecting fighter.
3. It travels away from the Deflecting fighter.
4. Its strike history resets in the same way as an already-repelled projectile.
5. It can then damage its previous owner.
6. Do not spawn a duplicate projectile.
7. Do not destroy it merely because it was Deflected.

There is already useful generic behavior in:

Projectile.turnBack(owner, direction)

0001’s Red uses the projectile repel mechanic and ultimately relies on this ownership/direction-reset concept. Reuse/refactor the generic behavior rather than implementing a second incompatible projectile reversal system.

Deflection ordering

This is important.

Current Arena ordering moves projectiles, resolves projectile clashes, then CombatSystem.update() handles melee followed by projectile hits.

Ensure Deflect-vs-projectile interaction occurs before that projectile can hit the fighter during the same fixed step.

For example:

active melee/deflect boxes
    ↓
deflect overlapping hostile projectile
    ↓
projectile owner/direction changes
    ↓
normal projectile-vs-fighter hit resolution

A projectile physically intercepted by an active Deflect should never simultaneously damage the Deflecting fighter because the projectile section happened later.

Direction

A Deflected projectile should leave the fighter rather than somehow being redirected into them.

For a forward-facing Deflect hitbox, fighter facing is a sensible outgoing direction.

If the implementation allows all-around Deflect hitboxes, determine the outgoing direction from the projectile’s position relative to the fighter so the projectile is always sent away from the fighter.

Projectile types

Deflection should work through the generic projectile system rather than being special-cased to Red, Blue, Whirlwind, etc.

Do not assume unblockable means undeflectable: blocking and deflecting are different mechanics.

Do not infer immunity simply because a projectile has repel, pierce, or erase.

If a future design needs an explicitly undeflectable projectile, that should be its own explicit data capability; do not silently create that exception as part of this task.

